// Lafayette Interior Fashions is named here and only here (rule 8 reserves
// the name for this page; Home and the services overview use "the lines I
// represent"). No logo or trademark is used, per rule 8.
//
// Naming Lafayette as the source is what rule 7 and 8.3 ask for. A formal
// relationship claim is not: the page said "Amy is a Lafayette Interior
// Fashions dealer" until 2026-07-26, which is an unconfirmed factual claim
// about a business relationship (BUILD-PLAN decision 12). Softened to "the one
// line I represent in this category," matching the holding pattern Stout and
// Pollack are already under. Restore the dealer wording only once Amy
// confirms it.
//
// Keyword cluster (8.3): "custom blinds Cincinnati, custom shades Northern
// Kentucky, Lafayette blinds Cincinnati" — title, H1, first paragraph, meta
// description.
//
// Roman shades appear as a searchable term but route to manufactured
// product, not Amy's workroom (5.3's own instruction) — the copy below says
// so explicitly rather than leaving it ambiguous.

import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import RevealImage from '../../components/RevealImage.astro';
import Accordion from '../../components/Accordion.astro';
import leadImage from '../../assets/images/portfolio/collected-living-room/banded-roman-shade-drapery-french-doors-detail.jpg';

const decisionCategories = [
  {
    title: 'Light and glare control',
    content:
      "How much light a room should keep is a different question from how much it lets in. A west-facing room with a television needs glare handled at one specific hour. A kitchen usually wants every bit of daylight it can hold. Solar shades are rated by openness, and that number decides how much view survives when they are down.",
  },
  {
    title: 'Privacy',
    content:
      'Privacy and light are not the same requirement, and treating them as one is the most common mistake here. A top-down/bottom-up shade covers the lower half of a window and leaves the daylight coming in above it, which is usually what a street-facing bedroom or bathroom actually needs. Sheers give daytime privacy and then read completely differently after dark, once the lamps are on inside.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Motorization',
    content:
      'Motorization earns its keep on tall windows, wide spans, and anything stranded above a stair or behind furniture, the treatments nobody ever adjusts because reaching them is a nuisance. It also lets a whole room drop on a schedule, ahead of the afternoon sun instead of after the room has already cooked. Battery, hardwired, and solar-charged suit different constructions, and the decision is far easier before drywall than after.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Child and pet safety',
    content:
      'Cordless, in any house with young children or pets. There is rarely a good reason to choose otherwise. Cordless lift, motorization, and tensioned continuous-loop mounts all take the hazard of a traditional pull cord off the table. Worth deciding on purpose rather than by omission.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Room by room',
    content:
      'Bathrooms and kitchens need something that shrugs off moisture and wipes clean. Bedrooms usually want room-darkening or blackout, often layered under drapery. Living rooms need something adjustable enough for a whole day of moving light. Woven woods warm up a room that reads flat. Shutters suit a window where the treatment should read as architecture rather than fabric.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Custom Blinds & Shades Cincinnati | Amy K Clark Design"
  description="Custom blinds Cincinnati and custom shades Northern Kentucky. Lafayette blinds, shades, shutters, and woven woods, measured and installed."
  ogImage="/images/og/og-default.jpg"
  transparentNav={false}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { name: 'Services', href: '/services' },
    { name: 'Blinds & Shades', href: '/services/blinds-shades' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="section wrap service-page__intro">
    <p class="type-label service-page__eyebrow">Services</p>
    <h1 class="type-display service-page__heading">Blinds &amp; Shades</h1>
    <p class="service-page__lead">
      Custom blinds in Cincinnati and custom shades across Northern Kentucky, specified for the way
      a room actually gets used. Lafayette Interior Fashions is the one line I represent in this
      category, so every Lafayette blind and shade here comes from a single catalog. One
      manufacturer, measured and installed by the person who helped choose it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <RevealImage
    src={leadImage}
    alt="Banded roman shade and pleated drapery framing French doors, with a crystal chandelier overhead"
    blurDataURL="data:image/webp;base64,UklGRqwAAABXRUJQVlA4IKAAAAAwBQCdASoYABUAPu1orlCppaQiqAqpMB2JZwAG0KCFeA8GvwPjvP6bHUT+YIQzyU7j0AD+FSHDV0ubeTAPHbf5413BYJW3pLc2ZHNK0MCBuh5vMwaNlvdw15DG9e1iBJcqwHR2FKUtXrkLqItBnK/DhKwBxjlP+OnZmgp6tjAHPHdy98FzAZDZkNeAJofae+wgRZ+DQT6E0ZUr/QTv/QAA"
    aspectRatio="16/9"
    sizes="100vw"
  />

  <section class="section wrap service-page__body">
    <h2 class="type-display service-page__subhead">One line, on purpose</h2>
    <p>
      Amy works from the Lafayette catalog rather than assembling treatments out of whichever
      supplier happens to be convenient that week. Blinds, shades, shutters, woven woods. Knowing
      one catalog cold produces better specifications than knowing six of them halfway.
    </p>
    <p>
      Roman shades live here as a manufactured product, built to Lafayette's specification rather
      than in Amy's workroom. When a room wants a fabric Roman shade made to order, a particular
      textile with a particular band, that is workroom work and it gets handled as custom drapery
      instead.
    </p>

    <h2 class="type-display service-page__subhead">Decide these before anything is ordered</h2>
    <p>
      Most regret about blinds and shades traces back to a decision nobody actually made. A shade
      picked for how it looked in a showroom, not for what the window does at four in the
      afternoon.
    </p>

    <Accordion items={decisionCategories} />

    <h2 class="type-display service-page__subhead">An eighth of an inch</h2>
    <p>
      Blinds and shades are unforgiving about measurement in a way drapery is not. An eighth of an
      inch decides whether an inside mount clears the frame or binds against it, and a mount depth
      that looked fine on paper can prove otherwise once the hardware is in hand. Amy measures
      every opening herself and installs the finished product herself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Windows in older Cincinnati and Northern Kentucky houses are almost never square. The
      allowances for that are a judgment call rather than a formula, and making them correctly is
      what keeps a treatment from looking almost right.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap contact-band bg-ground">
    <p class="type-display contact-band__line">Ready to start your project?</p>
    <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-solid">Schedule a Consultation</a>
  </section>
</Base>
